<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shajar al-Durr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1257">1257</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shajar al-Durr was the widow of the Ayyubid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub">al-Salih Ayyub</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as sultan in 1250. Her brief rule marked the transition from Ayyubid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> power. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> place her at its foundation, as she married the Mamluk commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a>, who became the first Mamluk sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr.jpg" alt="Shajar al-Durr" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stanley Lane Poole · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_sheger_ed_durr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shajar al-Durr, whose name means Tree of Pearls, was probably of Turkic or Armenian origin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub">As-Salih Ayyub</a> bought her as a slave in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> before he became sultan, and she accompanied him to Al Karak during his detention there in 1239. When he became sultan in 1240 she went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and bore their son Khalil, and he later married her.</p><p>Ayyub died on 22 November 1249 at Al Mansurah while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>'s crusader army was attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Shajar al-Durr, the commander Fakhr ad-Din and the chief eunuch Jamal ad-Din Muhsin concealed his death, forging documents in his hand, until his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a>. She agreed to Baibars's plan to defend Al Mansurah, where the crusader force was trapped and annihilated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a>'s arrival in February 1250 the crusaders were defeated at Fariskur on 6 April and Louis IX was captured. Turanshah then sought to replace his father's officials and demanded his father's wealth from Shajar al-Durr, which enraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. Baibars and other Mamluks assassinated him on 2 May 1250; he was the last of the Ayyubid sultans. The Mamluks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> installed Shajar al-Durr, with Izz al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a> as atabeg, and she took titles including Queen of the Muslims, invoking her husband and her son. Louis IX gave up Damietta and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> on 8 May 1250.</p><p>The Syrian Ayyubid emirs refused her homage, and the Abbasid Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> would not recognise a woman as ruler, writing that if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> had no men he would send some. After about three months she abdicated and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a>, who became sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1257 relations between Shajar al-Durr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a> had soured, and he decided to marry the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Shajar al-Durr had him murdered by servants while he bathed. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> did not believe her account of a sudden death, and she was arrested. On 28 April 1257 she was beaten to death by the bondmaids of Aybak's son al-Mansur Ali and his mother.</p><p>She built a tomb for her husband at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a> madrasa in 1250, joining tomb and madrasa, and a tomb for herself, whose mihrab carries a mosaic of a tree with pearl-like fruit recalling her name. She is regarded as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and she appears in the folk epic Sirat al-Zahir Baibars and in a 1935 film by Ahmed Galal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shajar al-Durr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shajar al-Durr</b>, al-Salih's widow, briefly ruled as sultan before opposition from the Abbasid caliph and Syrian Ayyubids led to her marriage to the commander Aybak, who became sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hatshepsut ruled Egypt as king; <b>Shajar al-Durr</b> ruled Egypt as sultan in 1250; Razia Sultana reigned in Delhi from 1236 to 1240; Tribhuwana and Suhita reigned in their own right in Majapahit; Yohl Ik'nal held the full title of holy lord at Palenque from 583 to 604 among…</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Many women of the ruling elite had been slaves themselves: the consorts of the Bahri sultans began as slave girls, and <b>Shajar al-Durr</b>, the widow of the Ayyubid sultan as-Salih Ayyub, ruled Egypt as sultan in 1250 before her marriage to the amir Aybak.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1247" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Shajar al-Durr"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1247">What was here in 1247? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
